summary is: {{ Patient 1: Christopher Watson, a 86-year-old male returns in follow-up for adult hydrocephalus. This is his first visit since undergoing sh- shunt surgery. The patient was established with a diagnosis of adult hydrocephalus with a spino catheter protocol in June 2020. He underwent shunt surgery on August 1, 2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration was programmed at a level 2.0 was placed. He had a history of eight to ten years of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. Since shunt surgery three months ago, has he been sick or been hosp -- hospitalized? No, he hasn't. He hasn't had any issues with his shunt. How are the symptoms? Are they better, worse, or unchanged? His walking has improved. He is now able to walk to the dining room just fine. He could not do that before surgery. He still has times where I have to help him. Now his bladder control did improve some after surgery, but now seems to have leveled out. So he is wearing diapers. No issues with sores from wearing the diaper? And any decreased or painful urination? No, not at all. And how about cognition? I think I'm better. I don't feel lost when talking to someone anymore. I think my sister, Hannah, summarized it best. She said " I feel like I can have a normal conversation with him again, " How about headaches or pain where the shunt is? No. Hasn't bothered me one bit. And any pain in your belly at all? Can't say that I have. Okay, sounds like you have been improving. Alrighty, Mr. Watson, I'm going to step out of the room while my resident comes in to do a few tests for me. Is that all right? Okay, I'm going to go out and review the ct scan you had last month, and then we'll chat about it when I come in. Alrighty, Mr. Watson, so you definitely have made some improvements since the shunt surgery. Now on ct scan though I do believe I see a tiny bit of blood fluid collection in the right parietal region. Now, I do believe that's causing any symptoms, and I, it actually wasn't documented by the radiologist when he read the ct scan. So really there's, there's nothing to worry about. I would like to leave the shunt set for another two to three months before we go in changing it. Like I said, if you're not having any headaches or pain and you have improved so that's all good for me. So really there's, there's nothing to worry about. I do have a question for you though. Do you think I will be able to drive again? Ah man, Mr. Watson, I knew you were gon na ask me that one. I think at this point it's unlikely that your movement speed will improve to the level that would be needed for you to pass the driver safety evaluation. Now, I will say that I do occasionally have patients who surprise me by improving over a nine to 12 month range. And that, that definitely could be you. You could definitely pass it at that point. So you could definitely enroll in a driver safety program without my recommendation. So you could kind of do that preemptively, and then we could continue to watch for your improvement as we go. I knew you were gon na ask me that one. I think your safety is our number one priority and, ya know, I'm sure your daughters don't mind as much, right, they want to keep you here and have you safe. But, I overall am very pleased with how much you have improved. And so, I'm sure when we talk again in three months, we'll continue to be on that upward trajectory or improvement. I sure hope so, doc. We really appreciate all that you've done. Do we make the appointment out front? Yeah, that would be great. The girls up front will take care of you and get you settled for three months from now, and you both take care, all right? Thank you. }}<|eot_id|>
