summary is: {{ Jeremy is a 67-year-old male who was moving boxes for his son who was moving out of his college dorm when he injured his back. He presented to the clinic four months after his injury. He had tried physical therapy at his gym and scheduled an appointment with me. He reported that he had pain with sitting, pain with movement for a very short period of time, and it felt like he was going to “tapple” over, he could not support his my upper body weight, and his right toe or the the toes on his right foot tingled a little bit but that went away after about an hour. He stated that the sensation has gone and he has not had any tingling or numbness since. He only had difficulty or weakness when he was carrying something heavy otherwise it seemed relatively normal but he felt the sensation so he was a little nervous about carrying something heavy. He also reported that if I turned a certain way or if I rolled over in his sleep he would wake up from it from a like a sharp pain but then that that went away if he if he positioned himself the right way. He took some tylenol, he took some ibuprofen, he’s tried heat, he’s tried ice and nothing really seems to help. He stated that the ibuprofen was a little bit better than the tylenol for sure a shorter period of time but he tries not to take too much so he i don't have an upset stomach from it. He went to an urgent care and they wanted him to do an mri but he was a little concerned because he thought we maxed out our insurance so he he didn't do it because it started to feel better but because it’s been going on so long he’s he’s a little bit concerned now he he does wan na actually move forward with that. He denied any incontinence, any problems with his bladder or his bowels, any difficulty with defecation, any difficulty with urination, any other symptoms. He had a knee surgery about two years ago on his right knee, that went fine, he rehabbed it pt has been fine it it feels about ninety five percent ever since. He had a total knee replacement in twenty eighteen, so it was actually about two years ago so it’s the spring of twenty twenty. On his physical exam, he had vital signs were good, he had no temperature, his blood pressure was good, it was about one seventeen over fifty six, his heart rate was nice and slow at sixty eight and his oxygenation was fine at ninety five percent on room air. On his neck exam, I i do appreciate some bony protuberance of the c5 c6. He had no pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. There was decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. The patient does have a positive straight leg raise and there is some spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine on musculoskeletal exam. The lower extremities strength was equal bilaterally. The patient had lumbar spine x-ray done before he saw me and I looked at the results of his lumbar spine x-ray which showed no acute bony abnormality. He had no fracture of his back or anything which is not surprising based on his history. So I do believe it sounds like he has an lumbar spine mri. }}<|eot_id|>
