summary is: {{ A 31-year-old male, Scott, came into the office complaining of a right knee injury. He stated that he had been playing soccer over the weekend and went to go plank kick the ball and felt a pop in his right knee. He stated that he had been active and had been jogging often. He also stated that he has a dog that he enjoys playing around with. He noticed some swelling, but some of that swelling had gone down. He stated that his knee is just not functioning as well as it used to be. It seems to be a little limited. He stated that the pain is ranging from a six to a seven on a scale of one to ten, one being the least pain and ten being the worst pain. He stated that the pain is about a four or five with some of the swelling going down. He took anti-inflammatories and stated that the ibuprofen helped a little bit but he is on it pretty on a pretty regular basis. He stated that he can bend it but it hurts towards the end of the motion and he can straighten it but it doesn't it feels like he has to help get it straightened. He stated that he can still bear weight on it but it feels just really loose it it doesn't feel normal. He had a marked limp on his looks like it's his right leg upon ambulation. Upon physical exam, we noticed a significant effusion in the right knee just a slight bit of ecchymosis. He felt some pain to palpation at the medial joint line. He felt some difficulty with passive range of motion. He lacked a few degrees of terminal extension to about a hundred and fifteen degrees as well as lacked about maybe twenty to thirty degrees of terminal flexion on the secondary to the pain. He had a positive lachman exam. We also evaluated the integrity of his toes and he had a muscle strength of a five out of five. Neurovascularly his bilateral lower extremities were intact. The right knee injury was really suggestive of anterior cruciate ligament tear. The plan was to go ahead and order an mri because we wanted to evaluate the integrity of that acl. He was told that he may need surgery but we would first take a look at that mri result and reevaluate and reevaluate that first. He was also told to go ahead and take anti-inflammatory medication, meloxicam fifty milligrams a day, and it's gon na help reduce that swelling and the pain. He was also told to ice the knee if he needed to if he was still seeing some swelling and some pain. He was told to moderate his activities so he would wear a knee immobilizer and then also use crutches when he would walk around just to help him a little bit so not to put too much stress and strain on that knee. He was also told that he would need to limit his work activities because he sits behind a desk but it's not been very comfortable sitting here lately. He was also told to do slight activities not enough to stress his legs but definitely move move them a little bit every now and then so it doesn't lock up on him. }}<|eot_id|>
