summary is: {{ Christina is a 35-year-old female who presented to the office with a nonhealing foot ulcer. She has type two diabetes mellitus which is the reason for her right ear but ulcer not healing. On further review of her history, it was revealed that the wound has been present for approximately one year. The patient stated that there was no trauma to the foot, but rather a small blister which became red and infected after she picked at it. She discussed the issue with her primary care provider (pcp) and was placed on antibiotics, which seemed to get better but nothing has really made it go away. On the initial visit to the office, she was on keflex for five months. The patient stated that she does not feel any pain, but she has no feeling in the foot. She does have neuropathy which makes sense with the the diabetes. She stated that there has been a lot more drainage lately and she feels more tired. On this visit, the patient is thursday, so she will be walking around at the zoo on this ulcer all weekend and eating out and not doing anything her doctor wants her to do. The patient also has diabetes mellitus and the pcp probably would not be excited about her eating out and all that. The patient was told that her diabetes is controlled but her last a1c was approximately ten. The patient stated that her blood sugars run better than they used to be but they are still in the two hundreds.
On exam, the patient had a temperature of 98.2 degrees F, heart rate of 88, respiratory rate of 18, and blood pressure of 123/74. The patient had no fever. On exam of the right foot, there was a one by two centimeter two inch circular wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, just proximal to the fifth mtp joint. There was yellow slough that was present with minimal granulation tissue. The patient had no surrounding erythema or cellulitis. There wasn't any evidence of fluid collection. There was no necrosis, or dead tissue, around it. There was no odor and the patient did not appreciate any bony exposure. On x-ray of the right foot, there was no evidence of osteomyelitis, or bone infection. The patient was assessed as having a diabetic foot ulcer. The plan was to get an abi or ankle brachial index, which is just to determine the blood supply just to see if we can actually heal that wound. It was explained to the patient that the wound has been there for about a year which is a pretty long time and so we just need to see if we can if the patient does have that blood flow just in case we need to to make other goals for that foot just to depend upon that. The patient was told that she would need a debridement here and she had that done before of the }}<|eot_id|>
