summary is: {{ Barbara is a 31 -year-old female with a history of diabetes type two and asthma who presented with abdominal pain. She reported that the abdominal pain had been present for one week and was gradually increasing in severity. Along with the abdominal pain, she also had nausea and vomiting on and off. She stated that she had experienced nausea for approximately three times in the past week. She also vomited a couple of times. She admitted to having a history of alcohol abuse and also a history of coffee intake. She reported that she had been drinking a lot of coffee lately, and she thought that it could have triggered her gastritis. She also reported that she had been exercising a lot lately and that her abdominal pain would increase when she worked out. She stated that she would like to jog or go for a run and her abdominal pain would just “spike” and she would have to take it easy. She also reported that she had not had any fever with that pain. She had a longstanding history of diabetes mellitus type two and was taking metformin 500 milligrams once a day. She was also on flovent 50 mcg twice a day and albuterol as needed for wheezing. She had not had any asthma attacks lately and thought she could decrease her flovent use. She reported that she had been drinking a lot of coffee, and she thought that it could have triggered her gastritis.
On physical exam, her heart exam was unremarkable with the exception of a grade three out of six systolic ejection murmur that was unchanged from her prior exam. She was not in any acute distress. She had a soft abdomen with mild tenderness in the epigastrium of her abdomen. Her abdomen was otherwise soft with no tenderness in the right lower quadrant. There was no significant tenderness in the right upper quadrant. She had mild end expiratory wheezes very faint and otherwise normal lung exam with excellent air movement. Her skin was warm and dry, and her extremities were well perfused. She had a normal mental status with appropriate speech, language, and cognition.
The assessment was acute gastritis, and the plan was to have her follow up in four weeks. She was told to cut back on the alcohol, alcohol, and caffeine. She was given a prescription for zantac as well as }}<|eot_id|>
