summary is: {{ Alexis is a 36 year old female who does data entry on a computer all day long. She sat at a keyboard for 8 hours a day, 5 days a week, and recently she started having some numbness and tingling in her right hand. She didn't know how long it was going on for, probably six months on and off, but over the last month or so it was a little more persistent. And she said she got some pain with it, but not a lot. And it was mostly when she was actually typing on the keyboard is when she experienced the pain. But the numbness and tingling was all the time. And she didn't know when it started. And when she got this pain, what made it better was stopping and just resting for a while, and she took medications, and she tried other things. And the other thing that made it hurt was her mouse. And her mouth, wow. And when she was moving that around. And does it ever wake her up in the middle of the night? No. And it's not affecting her sleep. And she's using motrin, ibuprofen, for for the pain. Well let me go ahead and take a take a look here at her is it both hands or just her right hand? Mostly her right hand. Mostly her right hand. Okay so let's go ahead and take a look at that right hand. When I bend her wrist does that cause any pain? Yes. It does. Okay and so you do have some pain on extension and if you pull your your hand wrist up towards you does that cause the pain also? Yes. Yeah okay so you do have pain on flexion and extension. And when I touch the fingertips here now she said she had some numbness in her thumb and her index finger and a little bit in her your middle finger. The other two fingers they feel okay or do you think that there is different than the other hand? Yeah for some reason it really is just that the thumb the pointer in the middle finger that she experiences the most symptoms with. Not why that is. Okay so I I want you to grip my hands here and just squeeze tight. Okay so you do have a a little bit of a less grip strength on the right and that's typical that we would see with this type of pain and and numbness that she has going on now I want you to turn your arm over for me and I'm gon na touch on or tap on the inside of your wrist or or forearm and when I do that I can see her face she's grimacing so that that must cause that shooting pain yeah okay so she does have a positive tinel's sign so in alexis what I think is going on is that she has a a carpal tunnel syndrome and we see that frequent but people who are doing data entry spend a lot of time on keyboards or you know some a lotta manufacturing people who do a repetitive procedure again and again will get that type of pain so here is my plan I'd like to put her in a splint and it's it's it's kind of a wrist splint I would like you to wear that at night that will help with that I want you to continue with the ibuprofens but I would like you to go up to six hundred milligrams four times a day and we're gon na do that now I am going to order some a a outpatient test called an emg where we look at that nerve conduction study in that wrist and that's really what's happening here is you've got some thickening in the the in the wrist and the the the membranes of the wrist that are just pressing down and just compressing some of those nerves any other questions for me for now if that test comes back positive and we'll talk about that later on her next visit if it comes back positive we may have to do we're gon na stick with conservative treatment first but we may consider some surgery where I can go in and release that impingement on her nerves through her wrist it's a simple outpatient procedure and then I think she would feel better how does that sound? That sounds like a good plan I was hoping maybe I could get some relief with some therapy rather than having to go to surgery so I like that plan very much. }}<|eot_id|>
