summary is: {{ Philip is a sixty-five-year-old male, who presented to the office complaining of right elbow pain. He also has a past medical history of hypertension and diabetes mellitus. He stated that he hurt his right elbow playing lacrosse with his kids. He was playing with a thirteen -year-old and he thinks he got hit with the lacrosse stick and hurt his elbow. He was also concerned about his high blood pressure and his diabetes mellitus.
Physical exam showed a blood pressure of one fifty over seven over ninety. He was on twenty of lisinopril. He had a grade two out of six systolic ejection murmur. He had a clear bilaterally lung exam. He had a normal heart exam. He had a bruise and swelling at the right elbow. He had pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. He had mild pain with flexion and extension. He had normal range of motion at the elbow. He had a normal x-ray of the right elbow. X-ray showed no fracture no bony abnormality. X-ray showed a normal joint space. X-ray showed a normal distal humerus epiphysis. X-ray showed a normal radial head and the coronoid process. X-ray showed no signs of an elbow dislocation. X-ray showed no signs of an elbow fracture.
The patient was given a diagnosis of elbow contusion. He was told to take eight hundred milligrams of ibuprofen twice a day. He was told to ice his elbow three times a day for twenty minutes at a time. He was told not to play lacrosse with his son for the next couple of weeks. He was given a prescription for ibuprofen. He was told to follow up in a couple of weeks. He was also told to continue his hypertension and diabetes mellitus medications.
The patient was seen again in the office two weeks later. He stated that his elbow was feeling much better. He was told to continue taking ibuprofen and icing his elbow. He was told to continue his hypertension and diabetes mellitus medications. He was told to follow up in a couple of months. He was given a prescription for ibuprofen. He was }}<|eot_id|>
